H2: MT5 White Label vs Grey Label
Both models give you a branded MT5 experience. The difference sits underneath, in control, cost and responsibility.
H3: MT5 White Label
 A white label generally gives you greater control over the platform. You have more freedom over configuration, trading conditions, group structures and how the environment is managed. Setup costs are higher and there's more operational responsibility, but you're building on a foundation that belongs to your business plan rather than someone else's.
H3: MT5 Grey Label
 A grey label operates under another provider's infrastructure and licensing arrangement. You're given a branded space within that provider's environment. Entry costs are lower and there's less to manage, which makes it attractive for lean launches. The trade-off is less flexibility over configuration and more dependence on the host provider's decisions.
H3: Which One Makes Sense?
 If you're planning for real volume, want to shape your own trading conditions, or expect to add products and markets over time, a white label is usually the stronger starting point. If you're testing a market, working with a tight budget or want the quickest possible route to a branded offering, a grey label may suit you better for now.
Plenty of brokers start on a grey label and move to a white label once the business justifies it. If that's your likely path, it's worth planning the CRM and integrations so the move is smooth when it comes.
